From 3bc88b8d19f54aa35af1e9abe27fbb38d4fe5820 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: claude-checkpoint <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 02:38:40 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 8/8] interpreter: fast paths in the tail-call dispatch on
 riscv64

On riscv64 a handler of the tail-call dispatch that calls anything, on
a cold path as well, saves the registers it keeps across the call when
it is entered and restores them when it leaves: 22 instructions in
MSTORE, around its own work. An instruction too large to be inlined
into its handler is called from it, and that costs the same again.

A fast path is the common case of an opcode, written without a call.
The handler tries it first. Where its conditions do not hold it has
changed nothing, and the handler leaves with a tail call to
dispatch_generic, which runs the instruction as any other is run, with
its checks and its failures. Six opcodes have one:

- MLOAD and MSTORE of a word at a multiple of 8 that the memory holds,
  or can be grown to hold in the room its buffer has. The growth is
  charged as Memory::resize_evm charges it.
- CALLDATALOAD of a word within the call data, at a multiple of 4.
- JUMP and a taken JUMPI end after the JUMPDEST they land on, whose
  gas they charge with their own: one dispatch less.
- PUSH2 runs the JUMP or JUMPI that follows it, which is how solc
  reaches a label. The target is the operand, so no word is written
  to the stack and read back, and one step takes the place of three.

The step leaves the pc, the stack and the gas as the separate handlers
do. Without the gas for all of it, with a full or an empty stack, or
with a target that is no JUMPDEST, the opcode runs alone and the next
handler reports what there is to report.

Off with an inspector, and for an opcode that a config overrides or
whose fast path runs one that it overrides. A table rebuilds the
handlers of PUSH2, JUMP and JUMPI when an opcode they run changes.
riscv64 only, and hosts built with --cfg evm2_as_on_riscv64.

Instructions a block in a Pico guest of RSP, before and after:
  13 mainnet blocks   155.667 M -> 149.793 M  (-3.77 %)
  3 Sepolia blocks    430.330 M -> 425.443 M  (-1.14 %)
  45 Sepolia blocks   536.225 M -> 527.649 M  (-1.60 %)
The guests commit the same values on all 61 blocks.

Tests, for every dispatch backend. Programs over the opcodes around a
jump against a model that runs them one opcode at a time: every case
of a PUSH2 with a jump after it, at every gas limit and at the stack
depths that decide (0, 1, 2, 1022, 1023, 1024), then 200,000 random
programs. The model counts the pairs by case and the tests require
every case. A digest over what the interpreter leaves behind is the
same for the tail-call dispatch with and without the fast paths and
for the packed table. 20,000 programs that store and load words at
offsets of every alignment, inside the memory and past its end,
against a memory kept as bytes, a quarter of them with the gas running
out at a random opcode. evm2's EEST runner passes its 28,647 fixtures
with the cfg and without.

